{:edb/db-before-t 1439 :edb/db-after-t 1440 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534752 50 #inst "2026-10-08T05:51:30.083Z" 13194139534752 true] [17592186133674 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307704 13194139534752 true] [17592186133675 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307690 13194139534752 true] [17592186133675 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307714 13194139534752 true] [17592186133676 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307697 13194139534752 true] [17592186133676 1020 #edb/ref 17592186307707 13194139534752 true] [17592186307690 1017 #edb/ref 17592186307691 13194139534752 true] [17592186307690 1089 "q-120712" 13194139534752 true] [17592186307690 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534752 true] [17592186307690 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040379/assets/2da1a7590ff9c8d403c040680c89f9bf310030d31399be7bd37209ef745a5bbd.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(4,-4)$, find the polar equation of the ray." 13194139534752 true] [17592186307690 1113 #uuid "5c407c7a-f52a-554c-8e95-8465a14c8781" 13194139534752 true] [17592186307690 1145 "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-4}{4}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the fourth quadrant. So, we find the angle in the fourth quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$" 13194139534752 true] [17592186307691 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307692 13194139534752 true] [17592186307691 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307693 13194139534752 true] [17592186307691 1000 #edb/ref 17592186307694 13194139534752 true] [17592186307691 1000 #edb